{"name":"Tabasco","hex":"#a02712","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Tabasco","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A state of Mexico.","A surname."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spicy pepper sauce made from tabasco pepper (a chili pepper) with the addition of vinegar and salt."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Tabasco"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tabasco","blocks":[{"text":"Tabasco, officially the Free and Sovereign State of Tabasco, is one of the 32 federal entities of Mexico. It is divided into 17 municipalities and its capital city is Villahermosa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Villahermosa_La_Venta_Park.JPG/330px-Villahermosa_La_Venta_Park.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tabasco"},{"title":"Tabasco (burlesque opera)","blocks":[{"text":"Tabasco is a \"burlesque opera in two acts\" with music by George W. Chadwick and a libretto by R. A. Barnet. The work is written in a blend of styles including operetta, musical theatre, Victorian burlesque, and vaudeville. The opera was created without the knowledge of McIlhenny Company, maker of Tabasco brand pepper sauce since 1868. The company, however, subsequently approved its performance. The opera went on to tour the U.S. to critical acclaim, but disagreements between Chadwick and the opera's producer eventually derailed its success."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/TabascoOpera2018.jpg/330px-TabascoOpera2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tabasco_(burlesque_opera)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Regions_of_tabasco-Es.png/500px-Regions_of_tabasco-Es.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Regi